{#  The LOOK, decided from the description.

    The screen used to ask an owner to pick a "front page shape" from
    three named skeletons and colours from a list including "the standard
    colours" -- internal vocabulary, and a question a person opening this
    for the first time has no way to answer. What they can describe is
    their business.

    So the model chooses, from THIS APP'S OWN vocabularies, which are
    listed below and are the only permitted answers. It is picking values
    the existing controls already carry -- never writing CSS, never
    inventing a font, never naming a shape this app does not have. The
    caller validates every one against the same lists and falls back
    rather than trusting; this prompt is the polite half of that. #}
You are choosing the visual identity for a small business website, from a
fixed set of options. You may not invent options.

The site:
{{ kit.brief }}

{% if kit.colour_note %}What they said about colour: {{ kit.colour_note }}
{% endif %}
{% if kit.tone_label %}How it should come across: {{ kit.tone_label|lower }}.
{% endif %}

The pages they want, in order — the first is the front page:
{% for title in pages %}{{ loop.index }}. {{ title }}
{% endfor %}

Choose:

1. Three colours as hex values — a primary, a secondary that sits with it, and
   an accent that genuinely contrasts. They must work as a set on a light page,
   and dark enough that white text on the primary is readable.
   {% if kit.colour_note %}Honour what they said about colour above.{% endif %}

{% if kit.ref_feel %}They showed you a picture of a site they like, and it reads as:
{{ kit.ref_feel }}. Let that guide the choice.

{% endif %}2. A typeface pairing, by key, from exactly this list:
{% for key, name in fonts %}   - {{ key }} — {{ name }}
